# Test artifacts — logs and runner output
tests/overnight_results/
tests/__pycache__/

# Benchmark datasets and results (large files)
tests/benchmark/data/
# Downloaded corpus pages. Re-fetchable from the rows API, ~27k rows for bitext,
# and slow to pull because that API rate-limits — kept on disk so a re-run is
# free, kept out of git because it is someone else's dataset.
tests/benchmark/.cache/
tests/benchmark/results/*
tests/mega/data/
tests/mega/data_1m/
tests/mega/reports/
tests/stress/*.jsonl

# Artifacts that back a decision are tracked by name. The negation list is the
# list of load-bearing artifacts: adding one is a deliberate statement that a
# claim somewhere depends on this file. Working output stays ignored.
#
# REMOVAL RULE, which is not the mirror of the addition rule: an artifact leaves
# this list when nothing cites it, NOT when it stops being current. Superseded
# and uncited are different conditions. inprocess_conversations150_rows.json.gz
# was dropped because nothing referenced it; inprocess_baseline_damp050* is
# superseded and stays, because two classification artifacts index into it.
# Check what cites a file before removing it, not whether a newer one exists.
#
# Note the directory is ignored as `results/*`, not `results/`. Git does not
# re-include a file inside an excluded directory, so negations under a `dir/`
# rule are silently inert — which is how this went unnoticed.
!tests/benchmark/results/accumulation_fit.json
!tests/benchmark/results/confidence_curve_fit.json
!tests/benchmark/results/inprocess_calibrated.json
!tests/benchmark/results/inprocess_damp050.json
!tests/benchmark/results/inprocess_damp100_at085.json
!tests/benchmark/results/threshold_sweep.json
!tests/benchmark/results/inprocess_conversations150.json
# Its row dump is deliberately NOT tracked. The 150-conversation run is a
# stability data point and that claim lives in its report; nothing is expected to
# re-derive from its rows. Row dumps are a deliberate act, not a per-run default.
#
# Two baseline row dumps are tracked, and that is intentional.
# inprocess_baseline_damp050* is superseded by inprocess_baseline_session_fields*
# but stays, because disagreement_classification.json and
# gate_candidate_classification.json both reference rows BY INDEX into
# inprocess_baseline_damp050_rows.json.gz. It is not dead weight. See the
# removal rule above.
!tests/benchmark/results/inprocess_baseline_damp050.json
!tests/benchmark/results/inprocess_baseline_damp050_rows.json.gz
!tests/benchmark/results/inprocess_baseline_session_fields.json
!tests/benchmark/results/inprocess_baseline_session_fields_rows.json.gz
!tests/benchmark/results/disagreement_classification.json
!tests/benchmark/results/gate_candidate_classification.json
!tests/benchmark/results/semantic_scoping_classification.json

# Support workload (bitext), four arms. Frozen and warm are not alternative
# runs of one thing — they bound the same cache cold and warmed, and quoting
# either alone misstates it. The held-out pair is the only measurement in this
# repo of serving when NOTHING correct is cached, and its frozen arm is kept
# despite reading 0/375 because that zero is uninformative on its own: the
# warm arm is what makes it mean something, and a reader needs both to see why.
!tests/benchmark/results/support_frozen_1500.json
!tests/benchmark/results/support_frozen_1500_rows.json.gz
!tests/benchmark/results/support_warm_1500.json
!tests/benchmark/results/support_warm_1500_rows.json.gz
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_1500.json
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_1500_rows.json.gz
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_warm_1500.json
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_warm_1500_rows.json.gz

# The serve_threshold sweep, LIVE. Three runs of the held-out warm arm at one
# commit and one tree hash, which is the whole point: the threshold changes what
# the cache contains, so the three cannot be recovered by re-reading one run's
# confidences against a different bar. Doing exactly that is what the
# threshold_sweep block inside each report does, and it understates wrong serves
# by 2.6x at 0.80. These three are the correction, and all three are needed: the
# 0.85 point is here rather than borrowed from support_heldout_warm_1500.json
# because that file predates the gate fix 3b90259 and reads 707 serves to these
# 709. Quoting it as this sweep's endpoint would compare across a code change.
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_warm_1500_at085.json
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_warm_1500_at085_rows.json.gz
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_warm_1500_at080.json
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_warm_1500_at080_rows.json.gz
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_warm_1500_at075.json
!tests/benchmark/results/support_heldout_warm_1500_at075_rows.json.gz

# The demo at the same three thresholds. Tracked because CONTEXT quotes 76% and
# 84% and, until now, demo.py wrote nothing at all, so both had a screenshot and
# a command behind them and no file. Read them for what they cannot show as much
# as what they can: new-unseen is 0/5 at every threshold, five samples that
# cannot distinguish 0% from the 42% the held-out arm measures at 0.80.
!tests/benchmark/results/demo_sweep_085.json
!tests/benchmark/results/demo_sweep_080.json
!tests/benchmark/results/demo_sweep_075.json

# The only artifact in this directory produced by REAL generation. Local
# inference, so it cannot speak to the README's hosted figures — it is kept
# because it is the sole provenance the cached-path numbers have.
!tests/benchmark/results/latency_local_ollama.json
# The same question asked over real HTTP through the gateway. Kept alongside
# the in-process one because the gap between them IS the HTTP stack, which is
# what the README's cached figure turns out to include.
!tests/benchmark/results/latency_http_gateway.json
# Two models over the same HTTP path. Both are kept because the pair IS the
# finding: the cold figure is a statement about the model, and quoting one
# without the other invites exactly the substitution this set exists to stop.
!tests/benchmark/results/latency_http_llama32_3b.json
!tests/benchmark/results/latency_http_llama31_8b.json
# Second 8B run, three days later, on a clean cache. Kept as a SECOND DATA
# POINT rather than a replacement: the pair is what makes the README's cached
# and speedup figures ranges instead of points, and it is the run the
# docs/latency_run.png screenshot shows.
!tests/benchmark/results/latency_http_llama31_8b_run2.json
# Third 8B run. Kept because it is the one that widened the published range:
# cached median 83.1ms and speedup 92.5x both fell outside what runs 1 and 2
# had established, so the README's figures are the spread across all three
# rather than a pair. Also the first artifact written after the speedup
# headline moved from means to medians, so it carries both bases.
!tests/benchmark/results/latency_http_llama31_8b_run3.json
# Fourth 8B run, and the first taken on a clean tree (provenance dirty=false).
# It widened the range again, cached median 88.5ms and speedup 90.0x, which is
# the point of keeping all four rather than the best pair: the spread is a
# property of the machine, not of any one run.
!tests/benchmark/results/latency_http_llama31_8b_run4.json

